<div class="section" id="recommendations">
  <h2>6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  <div class="section-subtitle">Data-driven actions for sourcing, pricing, and inventory decisions in the secondary smartwatch market.</div>

  <ul class="rec-list">
    <li>
      <div class="rec-num">1</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-title">Source Apple from the UK — the deepest discount market</div>
        <div class="rec-text">UK Apple listings median $395, a 28% discount to the US ($550) and 50% below Australia ($792). With 50% of UK sellers offering worldwide shipping, cross-border sourcing is operationally viable. Estimated margin: $150–$200 per unit after shipping and fees.</div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="rec-num">2</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-title">Arbitrage Garmin and Samsung from Japan</div>
        <div class="rec-text">Japan offers the lowest median prices for Garmin ($506, 11% below US) and Samsung ($437, 13% below US). With 62% worldwide shipping availability, Japanese listings are an accessible source. Target Canada and the UK as resale destinations where Garmin and Samsung command premiums of $890 and $825 respectively.</div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="rec-num">3</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-title">Prioritize Apple condition sensitivity in pricing models</div>
        <div class="rec-text">Apple's 17.2% new-vs-used premium is the only meaningful condition effect across the three brands. For Apple, condition-grade pricing should be a first-class variable in any pricing algorithm. For Garmin and Samsung, condition is nearly irrelevant — simplify the pricing model and avoid over-investing in condition verification for these brands.</div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="rec-num">4</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-title">Ignore case size as a pricing lever</div>
        <div class="rec-text">Case size correlation is negligible (r = 0.02 overall). Any observed size-based price differences reflect model-tier effects (Apple Ultra at 49mm, Garmin Epix at 51mm), not size premiums. Price by model, not by millimeter.</div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="rec-num">5</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-title">Partner with power sellers for inventory access — not pricing benchmarks</div>
        <div class="rec-text">Power sellers control 48.5% of listings but systematically charge 12–26% above casual seller prices. Their brand and condition mix (more Garmin, more New) explains much of this gap. When sourcing, casual sellers offer better unit economics; when seeking volume partnerships, power sellers provide inventory depth — but expect to pay a premium.</div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="rec-num">6</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-title">Diversify inventory beyond Apple to reduce concentration risk</div>
        <div class="rec-text">Apple represents 65.7% of all listings but shows the widest price variance (std $834, max $40,500) driven by Hermès and Ultra outliers. Garmin offers the most predictable resale value (std $198). A balanced portfolio with Garmin's price stability and Samsung's undersupplied presence (only 13% of listings) reduces exposure to Apple-specific price swings.</div>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

